<title>NETHERLANDS: FORECAST - Dutch see GDP rising 2.75 pct in 1997.</title>
<headline>FORECAST - Dutch see GDP rising 2.75 pct in 1997.</headline>
<dateline>THE HAGUE 1996-09-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Dutch government's Central Planning Bureau (CPB) published the following forecasts for the Dutch economy on Tuesday:</p>
<p>					    1997   1996   1995   1994</p>
<p>    Gross domestic product	  2.75   2.5     2.1    3.4</p>
<p>    Private consumption	     1.75   2.5     2.1    2.2</p>
<p>    Gross business investment     2.5    4.5    10.0   -0.9</p>
<p>    Business production	     3.0    2.5     2.2    3.6</p>
<p>    Exports			     6.0    4.5     8.6    8.7</p>
<p>    Imports			     5.25   4.75    8.3    7.6</p>
<p>    Contract wages		    2.5    1.5     1.4    1.8</p>
<p>    Consumer price index	    2.5    2.25    2.0    2.7</p>
<p>    Current account surplus	 5.5    5.0     5.1    5.1</p>
<p>    Unemployment A		    465    500     533    547</p>
<p>    Unemployment B		    5.25   6.0     6.5    6.8</p>
<p>    Dollar rate in guilders	 1.70   1.68    1.61   1.82</p>
<p>    10-yr Dutch govt bond yield   6.25   6.5     6.9    6.9</p>
<p>NOTES - All figures refer to real percentage changes versus previous year unless otherwise stated below. CBS rounds off its estimates to nearest 0.25 percent.</p>
<p>Gross business investment excludes housing. Exports exclude energy. Current account surplus is expressed as percentage of GDP. Unemployment A is according to domestic definition in thousands of people. Unemployment B is according to standardised international definition as percentage of labour force.</p>
<p>-- Amsterdam newsroom +31 20 504 5000, Fax +31 20 504 5040</p>
</text>
